---
// What stays and every network call, from the README's Privacy section ("Every network call, listed")
// and SECURITY.md. Checked against the 1.0 source: the model rows' download URLs (ink-engines'
// registry, fetched only when the user presses Download), the Mac's update feed and
// SUEnableSystemProfiling (mac/Info.plist), a dictation keeping no audio (ink-pipeline), the
// retention choices (retention.days), the language-model consents
// (ink_pipeline::consent) and Windows's providers and key store (ink_llm). Windows's update row follows
// the updater its distribution step builds: nothing is checked until the user asks. This section
// doubles as the privacy policy the code signing policy links.
import { GITHUB_URL, HUGGING_FACE_URL, RELEASES_URL } from '../lib/constants';
---

<section class="privacy wrap" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="privacy-title">What it sends, all of it.</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      No account, no telemetry, no analytics, no crash reporting, and no server of this project’s that your
      words pass through. Here is what stays, and every call that goes out.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group">
    <h3>Stays on this machine</h3>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div>
        <dt>Your voice</dt>
        <dd>
          Transcribed on this machine, never uploaded. A dictation’s audio is not kept. A meeting’s audio is,
          on disk beside its record, because the final pass and the playback read it from there, until your
          Keep records setting removes it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Your transcripts</dt>
        <dd>
          Dictations, meetings, notes and summaries, in a SQLite file in the app’s data folder. Nothing
          syncs, and no log ever holds your words.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The microphone</dt>
        <dd>
          For dictation it opens when you press the key and is let go of a minute after your last take. For a
          meeting it is open while you record, and recording starts only when you say so.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Your calendar</dt>
        <dd>
          On the Mac, only if you allow it: read to show your next meeting and to name each meeting and who was
          in it. It never leaves the Mac.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Language models</dt>
        <dd>
          On the Mac, polish, voice edit, summaries and Ask run on Apple’s on-device model, so your words stay
          on the Mac. On Windows they need a provider you set up with your own API key, and send it the words
          they work on (the table below); the key stays in Windows Credential Manager. Each is off until you
          allow it, and asks again if where your words would go ever changes.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="spec-group is-table">
    <h3 id="calls-title">Every network call</h3>
    <div class="ledger-wrap">
      <table class="ledger" role="table" aria-labelledby="calls-title">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Call</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Goes to</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">When</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">What it carries</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Speech model download</th>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">Goes to</span><a href={HUGGING_FACE_URL}>Hugging Face</a></td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">When</span>When you press Download, in the first run or in Settings; never before</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">What it carries</span>A request for the model’s files, at a pinned revision; each file is checked against its hash</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Voice-detection model download</th>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">Goes to</span>GitHub, the Silero VAD repository</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">When</span>With the speech models, when you press Download</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">What it carries</span>A request for that one file, checked the same way</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Update check, Mac</th>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">Goes to</span>This project’s <a href={RELEASES_URL}>GitHub releases</a></td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">When</span>Only once you have said yes to the updater’s question, and when you check by hand</td>
            <td role="cell">
              <span class="m-only">What it carries</span>A request for the update feed. Nothing about your Mac: no
              system profile is sent
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Updates, Windows</th>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">Goes to</span>This project’s <a href={RELEASES_URL}>GitHub releases</a></td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">When</span>Only when you ask the app to check</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">What it carries</span>A request for the release feed, then for the update if you take it. Nothing about your PC</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Language model, Windows</th>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">Goes to</span>The provider you set up: OpenAI, Anthropic, Groq, OpenRouter, or a server you name</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">When</span>Only if you set one up: when a feature you allowed for it runs, or when you test it</td>
            <td role="cell"><span class="m-only">What it carries</span>The words that feature works on (a dictation, the text you selected, a meeting’s transcript, your question) and your API key. A test carries a fixed question, none of your words</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="after-ledger">
      With the models on disk, dictation and meetings work with the network unplugged; what is left is
      updates and, on Windows, a provider you set up. Everything that decides what is sent is in the <a href={GITHUB_URL}>source</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
